<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zanabazar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Öndör Gegeen · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zanabazar was a Mongolian Buddhist leader and artist, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gombodorj">Tüsheet Khan Gombodorj</a> and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebtsundamba-khutughtu">Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</a>, head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, he founded monasteries and in 1686 devised the Soyombo script. In 1691 he led the Khalkha princes in submitting to the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a>. His gilded bronze sculptures of Buddhist deities are regarded as masterpieces of Mongolian art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zanabazar.jpg" alt="Zanabazar" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zanabazar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zanabanzar_self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zanabazar was born Eshidorji in 1635 in present-day Yesönzüil, Övörkhangai, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gombodorj">Gombodorj</a> and his wife Khandojamtso. His father traced his line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan">Abtai Sain Khan</a>, who had opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> lands to Gelugpa Buddhism. In 1639 an assembly of Khalkha nobles at Shireet Tsagaan nuur recognised the four-year-old as Öndör Gegeen, high saint and supreme religious leader of the Khalkha, a choice that gave the nobility added religious legitimacy.</p><p>In 1649 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> to study under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> and the 4th Panchen Lama at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drepung-monastery">Drepung Monastery</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> identified him as the reincarnation of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranatha">Taranatha</a> and gave him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name Jñānavajra, from which Zanabazar comes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1651 and 1656 he founded Gelug-influenced monasteries, temples and shrines across Mongol territory, among them the Saridgiin Monastery in the Khentii mountains, completed in 1680. His travelling residence, the Örgöö, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a>, eventually became the capital Ulaanbaatar. He revived metal image making in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and his workshop at Tövkhön Monastery produced works such as the White Tara of 1685 and the Five Dhyani Buddhas.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan">Galdan Boshugtu Khan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> in 1688, Zanabazar fled south with nearly 20,000 refugees to seek the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>, and Dzungar forces pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a>. After the submission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a> he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until 1701, wintering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and spending summers with the emperor at Jehol as his spiritual mentor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> in 1701 to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a>, then journeyed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>'s death and died there on 18 February 1723; some believe he was poisoned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> ordered Amarbayasgalant Monastery built in his honour, where his body was laid to rest in 1779. Communists ransacked it in 1937, and his remains were allegedly burned. Socialist authorities portrayed him as a traitor for the submission to the Qing, a view since revised, and the Fine Arts Zanabazar Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> holds the largest collection of his works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zanabazar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Zanabazar</b>, son of Tüsheet Khan Gombodorj, became the first Jebtsundamba Khutughtu, the head of Mongolian Buddhism, as well as a famous sculptor.</blockquote><a href="/state/tusheet-khanate">Tüsheet Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yesönzüil, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.62,103.65&amp;year=1692" aria-label="Yesönzüil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6988 525 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zanabazar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7438 795h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7438 795h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.62,103.65&amp;year=1692">What was here in 1692? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
